    <item>
      <title>Icahn’s Fortune Sinks to $1.3b as Debt Pressures Rise</title>
      <link>https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2026-11-24/icahn-s-fortune-sinks-to-1-3b-as-debt-pressures-rise-video</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">7aa5ebc3d28419e660b03013</guid>
      <pubDate>Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:29:33 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>Carl Icahn’s fortune has dwindled to about $1.3 billion as mounting debt squeezes his business empire, leaving the longtime corporate raider and activist investor facing one of the toughest stretches of his career. When Icahn Enterprises reported third-quarter earnings this month, it warned there was no assurance it could refinance $1.4 billion of bonds due in May on acceptable terms — or at all. Since then, the pressure has only intensified. Shares of CVR Energy, a key IEP holding, have tumbled about 40% since it agreed earlier this month to buy two additional refineries, eroding the value of a stake that accounted for about half of Icahn’s publicly traded investment partnership at the end of September. Yields on some of IEP’s 2029 bonds now exceed 30%, and the company has about $4.4 billion of bonds outstanding. Tom Maloney, Bloomberg News Editor, discusses how lots of leverage of led to Carl Icahn's investment losses. (Source: Bloomberg)</description>
